WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:02.776 --> 00:04.796 align:middle
aTranscript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference September 20,

00:04.796 --> 00:05.726 align:middle
2023 CHAIR POWELL.

00:05.936 --> 00:06.606 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:06.606 --> 00:08.976 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:08.976 --> 00:11.726 align:middle
on our dual mandate to
promote maximum employment

00:11.896 --> 00:13.776 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

00:14.986 --> 00:17.766 align:middle
We understand the hardship
that high inflation is causing,

00:18.076 --> 00:21.526 align:middle
and we remain strongly committed
to bringing inflation back

00:21.526 --> 00:22.926 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal.

00:24.736 --> 00:26.386 align:middle
Price stability is
the responsibility

00:26.386 --> 00:27.276 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve.

00:28.556 --> 00:29.786 align:middle
Without price stability,

00:29.896 --> 00:31.806 align:middle
the economy does
not work for anyone.

00:32.166 --> 00:34.026 align:middle
In particular, without
price stability,

00:34.586 --> 00:36.406 align:middle
we will not achieve
a sustained period

00:36.406 --> 00:38.896 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

00:40.816 --> 00:44.516 align:middle
Since early last year, the FOMC
has significantly tightened the

00:44.516 --> 00:45.926 align:middle
stance of monetary policy.

00:46.916 --> 00:51.016 align:middle
We've raised our policy interest
rate by 5¼ percentage points

00:51.436 --> 00:53.886 align:middle
and have continued to reduce
our securities holdings

00:53.886 --> 00:54.886 align:middle
at a brisk pace.

00:55.996 --> 00:58.066 align:middle
We've covered a lot of
ground, and the full effects

00:58.066 --> 00:59.566 align:middle
of our tightening
have yet to be felt.

01:01.426 --> 01:04.646 align:middle
Today, we decided to leave our
policy interest rate unchanged

01:04.836 --> 01:07.326 align:middle
and to continue to reduce
our securities holdings.

01:08.646 --> 01:11.506 align:middle
Looking ahead, we're in a
position to proceed carefully

01:11.906 --> 01:14.496 align:middle
in determining the extent
of additional policy firming

01:14.496 --> 01:15.696 align:middle
that may be appropriate.

01:16.256 --> 01:20.076 align:middle
Our decisions will be based
on our ongoing assessments

01:20.076 --> 01:23.516 align:middle
of the incoming data and the
evolving outlook and risks.

01:23.676 --> 01:26.486 align:middle
I will have more to say
about monetary policy

01:26.486 --> 01:29.406 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:31.226 --> 01:32.536 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest

01:32.536 --> 01:36.666 align:middle
that economic activity has been
expanding at a solid pace, and,

01:36.666 --> 01:39.686 align:middle
so far this year, growth
in real GDP has come

01:39.686 --> 01:41.076 align:middle
in above expectations.

01:42.716 --> 01:44.636 align:middle
Recent readings on
consumer spending have been

01:44.636 --> 01:46.146 align:middle
particularly robust.

01:47.496 --> 01:49.866 align:middle
Activity in the housing
sector has picked up somewhat,

01:50.416 --> 01:52.926 align:middle
though it remains well
below levels of a year ago,

01:53.096 --> 01:55.096 align:middle
largely reflecting
higher mortgage rates.

01:56.446 --> 01:58.536 align:middle
Higher interest rates
also appear to be weighing

01:58.536 --> 01:59.926 align:middle
on business fixed investment.

02:01.226 --> 02:03.746 align:middle
In our Summary of Economic
Projections, or SEP,

02:03.746 --> 02:07.296 align:middle
Committee participants
revised up their assessments

02:07.296 --> 02:09.866 align:middle
of real GDP growth,
with the median

02:09.866 --> 02:12.516 align:middle
for this year now
at 2.1 percent.

02:14.036 --> 02:16.356 align:middle
Participants expect
growth to cool,

02:16.356 --> 02:18.116 align:middle
with the median projection
falling

02:18.116 --> 02:19.806 align:middle
to 1.5 percent next year.

02:20.396 --> 02:23.556 align:middle
The labor market
remains tight, but supply

02:23.556 --> 02:26.276 align:middle
and demand conditions continue
to come into better balance.

02:27.046 --> 02:28.316 align:middle
Over the past three months,

02:28.396 --> 02:31.716 align:middle
payroll job gains averaged
150,000 jobs per month,

02:32.356 --> 02:35.106 align:middle
a strong pace that is
nevertheless well below

02:35.106 --> 02:36.476 align:middle
that seen earlier in the year.

02:37.556 --> 02:39.916 align:middle
The unemployment rate
ticked up in August

02:39.946 --> 02:42.216 align:middle
but remains low at 3.8 percent.

02:42.736 --> 02:45.676 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate has moved

02:45.676 --> 02:47.876 align:middle
up since late last
year, particularly

02:47.876 --> 02:51.456 align:middle
for individuals aged
25 to 54 years.

02:53.066 --> 02:55.716 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
shown some signs of easing,

02:56.266 --> 02:58.636 align:middle
and job vacancies have
declined so far this year.

02:59.946 --> 03:02.726 align:middle
Although the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed,

03:03.186 --> 03:06.716 align:middle
labor demand still exceeds the
supply of available workers.

03:07.196 --> 03:11.866 align:middle
FOMC participants expect the
rebalancing in the labor market

03:11.866 --> 03:15.446 align:middle
to continue, easing upward
pressures on inflation.

03:16.056 --> 03:20.526 align:middle
The median unemployment rate
projection in the SEP rises

03:20.526 --> 03:24.426 align:middle
from 3.8 percent at the end
of this year to 4.1 percent

03:24.496 --> 03:25.686 align:middle
over the next two years.

03:28.076 --> 03:31.446 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

03:32.676 --> 03:35.826 align:middle
Based on the consumer price
index, or CPI, and other data,

03:35.826 --> 03:39.716 align:middle
we estimate that total PCE
prices rose 3.4 percent

03:40.116 --> 03:42.836 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in August and that,

03:42.836 --> 03:45.586 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:45.936 --> 03:48.776 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
3.9 percent.

03:50.366 --> 03:53.176 align:middle
Inflation has moderated somewhat
since the middle of last year,

03:53.556 --> 03:56.766 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations appear

03:56.766 --> 03:59.826 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

03:59.826 --> 04:03.296 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

04:03.776 --> 04:05.696 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

04:06.736 --> 04:09.386 align:middle
Nevertheless, the
progress-process

04:09.386 --> 04:11.016 align:middle
of getting inflation sustainably

04:11.016 --> 04:13.356 align:middle
down to 2 percent
has a long way to go.

04:14.516 --> 04:16.516 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

04:16.516 --> 04:20.676 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
is 3.3 percent this year,

04:20.986 --> 04:26.006 align:middle
falls to 2.5 percent next year,
and reaches 2 percent in 2026.

04:26.516 --> 04:29.746 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

04:29.746 --> 04:32.066 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:32.266 --> 04:34.256 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

04:35.366 --> 04:37.426 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

04:37.566 --> 04:39.946 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

04:39.946 --> 04:44.556 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

04:44.556 --> 04:46.996 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

04:47.426 --> 04:48.846 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

04:49.416 --> 04:53.406 align:middle
We are highly attentive to the
risks that high inflation poses

04:53.406 --> 04:56.496 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we're strongly committed

04:56.496 --> 04:58.976 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

05:01.236 --> 05:03.426 align:middle
As I noted earlier,
since early last year,

05:03.426 --> 05:06.636 align:middle
we have raised our policy
rate by 5¼ percentage points.

05:07.516 --> 05:10.466 align:middle
We see the current stance of
monetary policy as restrictive,

05:11.166 --> 05:13.606 align:middle
putting downward pressure
on economic activity,

05:13.746 --> 05:14.926 align:middle
hiring, and inflation.

05:16.016 --> 05:18.846 align:middle
In addition, the economy
is facing headwinds

05:18.846 --> 05:21.726 align:middle
from tighter credit conditions
for households and businesses.

05:23.056 --> 05:25.566 align:middle
In light of how far we have
come in tightening policy,

05:25.926 --> 05:27.706 align:middle
the Committee decided
at today's meeting

05:28.176 --> 05:31.586 align:middle
to maintain the target range for
the federal funds rate at 5¼

05:31.586 --> 05:34.916 align:middle
to 5½ percent and to
continue the process

05:34.916 --> 05:37.326 align:middle
of significantly reducing
our securities holdings.

05:37.976 --> 05:41.606 align:middle
We are committed to achieving
and sustaining a stance

05:41.606 --> 05:43.996 align:middle
of monetary policy that is
sufficiently restrictive

05:44.066 --> 05:46.896 align:middle
to bring inflation down to
our 2 percent goal over time.

05:48.206 --> 05:52.186 align:middle
In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

05:52.186 --> 05:53.606 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

05:53.606 --> 05:55.736 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

05:55.966 --> 05:57.936 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

05:57.936 --> 06:05.166 align:middle
to be the most likely-sorry-the
most likely scenario

06:05.166 --> 06:05.786 align:middle
going forward.

06:06.336 --> 06:08.116 align:middle
If the economy evolves
as projected,

06:08.256 --> 06:10.856 align:middle
the median participant projects
that the appropriate level

06:10.856 --> 06:14.146 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 5.6 percent at the end

06:14.146 --> 06:18.036 align:middle
of this year, 5.1 percent
at the end of 2024,

06:18.446 --> 06:21.596 align:middle
and 3.9 percent at
the end of 2025.

06:23.146 --> 06:26.026 align:middle
Compared with our June Summary
of Economic Projections,

06:26.026 --> 06:28.416 align:middle
the median projection
is unrevised for the end

06:28.416 --> 06:32.076 align:middle
of this year but has moved up by
½ percentage point at the end

06:32.076 --> 06:33.296 align:middle
of the next two years.

06:34.476 --> 06:38.146 align:middle
These projections, of course,
are not a Committee decision

06:38.146 --> 06:41.146 align:middle
or plan; if the economy does
not evolve as projected,

06:41.146 --> 06:43.416 align:middle
the path of policy will
adjust as appropriate

06:44.006 --> 06:46.956 align:middle
to foster our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

06:47.476 --> 06:50.886 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting,

06:51.156 --> 06:53.066 align:middle
based on the totality
of the incoming data

06:53.066 --> 06:56.176 align:middle
and their implications for the
outlook for economic activity

06:56.176 --> 06:58.696 align:middle
and inflation, as well
as the balance of risks.

06:59.996 --> 07:02.356 align:middle
Given how far we have
come, we are in a position

07:02.356 --> 07:05.256 align:middle
to proceed carefully as we
assess the incoming data

07:05.596 --> 07:07.426 align:middle
and the evolving
outlook and risks.

07:08.476 --> 07:11.356 align:middle
Real interest rates now are
well above mainstream estimates

07:11.356 --> 07:14.646 align:middle
of the neutral policy
rate, but we are mindful

07:14.646 --> 07:15.996 align:middle
of the inherent uncertainties

07:16.316 --> 07:18.386 align:middle
in precisely gauging
the stance of policy.

07:19.386 --> 07:21.956 align:middle
We're prepared to raise
rates further if appropriate,

07:22.756 --> 07:25.086 align:middle
and we intend to hold policy
at a restrictive level

07:25.216 --> 07:27.886 align:middle
until we're confident
that inflation is moving

07:28.086 --> 07:30.176 align:middle
down sustainably
toward our objective.

07:30.756 --> 07:34.076 align:middle
In determining the extent
of additional policy firming

07:34.076 --> 07:36.916 align:middle
that may be appropriate to
return inflation to 2 percent

07:36.916 --> 07:39.206 align:middle
over time, the Committee
will take

07:39.206 --> 07:42.206 align:middle
into account the cumulative
tightening of monetary policy,

07:42.946 --> 07:46.786 align:middle
the lags with which monetary
policy affects economic activity

07:46.786 --> 07:50.356 align:middle
and inflation, and economic
and financial developments.

07:51.836 --> 07:53.996 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

07:53.996 --> 07:55.366 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

07:55.566 --> 07:58.156 align:middle
and to keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

07:58.206 --> 07:58.806 align:middle
well anchored.

08:00.036 --> 08:02.666 align:middle
Reducing inflation is
likely to require a period

08:02.666 --> 08:05.146 align:middle
of below-trend growth
and some softening

08:05.146 --> 08:06.416 align:middle
of labor market conditions.

08:08.076 --> 08:11.166 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

08:11.166 --> 08:12.646 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

08:12.716 --> 08:14.766 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

08:16.446 --> 08:19.666 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

08:20.016 --> 08:21.886 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

08:22.816 --> 08:25.266 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

08:26.326 --> 08:28.426 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:28.476 --> 08:29.996 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:29.996 --> 08:31.246 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:32.066 --> 08:34.156 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

08:34.876 --> 08:35.996 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:37.046 --> 08:38.826 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

08:39.616 --> 08:41.096 align:middle
Thank you.

08:41.096 --> 08:42.606 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

08:43.206 --> 08:44.986 align:middle
What makes the Committee
inclined to think

08:45.076 --> 08:47.566 align:middle
that the fed funds rate
at this level is not

08:47.566 --> 08:48.986 align:middle
yet sufficiently restrictive,

08:49.356 --> 08:52.676 align:middle
especially when officials are
forecasting a slightly more

08:52.966 --> 08:54.926 align:middle
benign inflation
outlook for this year?

08:55.556 --> 08:58.176 align:middle
There's noted uncertainty
about policy lags.

08:58.516 --> 09:02.736 align:middle
Headwinds have emerged from the
looming government shutdown,

09:03.016 --> 09:04.876 align:middle
the end of federal
childcare funding,

09:05.136 --> 09:08.816 align:middle
resumption of student debt
payments-things of that nature.

09:09.516 --> 09:10.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:10.286 --> 09:12.596 align:middle
So I guess I would
characterize the,

09:12.686 --> 09:14.736 align:middle
the situation a little
bit differently.

09:15.336 --> 09:17.496 align:middle
So we decided to
maintain the target range

09:17.496 --> 09:19.516 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate where it is-at 5¼

09:19.516 --> 09:22.006 align:middle
to 5½ percent-while continuing

09:22.006 --> 09:23.596 align:middle
to reduce our securities
holdings.

09:24.246 --> 09:25.846 align:middle
And we say we're
committed to achieving

09:25.846 --> 09:27.256 align:middle
and sustaining a stance

09:27.256 --> 09:29.196 align:middle
of monetary policy that's
sufficiently restrictive

09:29.196 --> 09:31.596 align:middle
to bring down inflation to 2
percent over time-we said that.

09:32.496 --> 09:37.966 align:middle
But the fact that we decided
to maintain the policy rate

09:37.966 --> 09:41.696 align:middle
at this meeting doesn't mean
that we've decided that we have

09:41.696 --> 09:46.126 align:middle
or have not at this time
reached that-that stance

09:46.126 --> 09:47.766 align:middle
of monetary policy
that we're seeking.

09:48.486 --> 09:49.556 align:middle
If you looked at the SEP,

09:49.556 --> 09:51.576 align:middle
as you-as you obviously
will have done, you will see

09:51.576 --> 09:55.046 align:middle
that a majority of participants
believe that it is more likely

09:55.046 --> 09:58.676 align:middle
than not that we will-that
it will be appropriate for us

09:58.676 --> 10:00.356 align:middle
to raise rates one more time

10:01.206 --> 10:03.306 align:middle
in the two remaining
meetings this year.

10:04.336 --> 10:07.136 align:middle
Others believe that we
have already reached that.

10:07.136 --> 10:08.886 align:middle
So it's, it's something
where we're-by,

10:09.036 --> 10:13.186 align:middle
by-we're not making a decision
by, by deciding to-about

10:13.186 --> 10:15.706 align:middle
that question by deciding
to just maintain the rate

10:15.706 --> 10:16.766 align:middle
and await further data.

10:17.826 --> 10:18.276 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

10:18.276 --> 10:20.446 align:middle
So, right now, it's still
an open question about

10:20.446 --> 10:22.036 align:middle
"sufficiently
restrictive"-you're not saying

10:22.066 --> 10:23.766 align:middle
today that we've
reached this level?

10:23.766 --> 10:24.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:24.536 --> 10:26.416 align:middle
We're not saying-yeah,
no, no-clearly,

10:26.416 --> 10:29.036 align:middle
we're just-what we decided to
do is maintain a policy rate

10:29.036 --> 10:30.096 align:middle
and await further data.

10:30.686 --> 10:32.776 align:middle
We want to see convincing
evidence, really,

10:33.096 --> 10:35.516 align:middle
that we have reached the
appropriate level, and then,

10:35.566 --> 10:37.546 align:middle
you know, we're-we've-we've
seen progress,

10:37.546 --> 10:38.746 align:middle
and, and we welcome that.

10:39.406 --> 10:40.816 align:middle
But, you know, we need

10:40.816 --> 10:43.106 align:middle
to see more progress before
we'll be willing to-to reach

10:43.886 --> 10:45.336 align:middle
that conclusion.

10:45.496 --> 10:45.866 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

10:45.866 --> 10:48.876 align:middle
And just on the 2024
projections-what's behind

10:48.876 --> 10:52.056 align:middle
that shallower path for
interest rate cuts and the need

10:52.056 --> 10:54.066 align:middle
for real rates to be
50 basis points higher?

10:54.356 --> 10:55.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:55.026 --> 10:59.476 align:middle
Right. So I would say it
this way: First of all,

11:00.576 --> 11:03.536 align:middle
interest rates-real interest
rates are, are positive now.

11:03.596 --> 11:06.326 align:middle
They're meaningfully positive,
and that's a good thing.

11:06.326 --> 11:08.326 align:middle
We need policy to be restrictive

11:08.696 --> 11:10.426 align:middle
so that we can get
inflation down to target.

11:10.616 --> 11:13.526 align:middle
Okay. And we need-we're
going to need that to remain

11:13.526 --> 11:16.086 align:middle
to be the case for some time.

11:16.086 --> 11:18.746 align:middle
So I think, you know-remember
that the-of course,

11:18.746 --> 11:21.526 align:middle
the SEP is not a plan
that is negotiated

11:21.526 --> 11:22.966 align:middle
or discussed, really, as a plan.

11:22.966 --> 11:25.956 align:middle
It's a cumulation, really, and
what you see are the medians.

11:25.956 --> 11:28.686 align:middle
It's a cumulation of individual
forecasts from 19 people,

11:28.796 --> 11:30.416 align:middle
and then what you're
seeing are the medians.

11:30.416 --> 11:33.616 align:middle
So I wouldn't want to, you know,
bestow upon it the idea that,

11:33.616 --> 11:34.666 align:middle
that it's really a plan.

11:34.666 --> 11:36.116 align:middle
But what it reflects, though,

11:36.116 --> 11:38.696 align:middle
is that economic
activity's been stronger

11:38.696 --> 11:41.226 align:middle
than we expected-stronger than
I think everyone expected.

11:41.716 --> 11:44.186 align:middle
And, and so what
you're-what you're seeing is,

11:44.186 --> 11:48.436 align:middle
this is what people believe,
as of now, will be appropriate

11:48.786 --> 11:50.726 align:middle
to achieve what we're
looking to achieve,

11:50.726 --> 11:54.826 align:middle
which is progress toward
our-toward our inflation goal,

11:54.986 --> 11:55.816 align:middle
as you see in the SEP.

11:55.816 --> 11:56.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:57.496 --> 11:58.746 align:middle
Thanks. Let's go to Rachel.

11:58.746 --> 12:02.006 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

12:03.076 --> 12:03.946 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

12:04.016 --> 12:05.576 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

12:05.736 --> 12:06.836 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

12:07.886 --> 12:09.856 align:middle
How would you characterize
the debate

12:09.856 --> 12:12.436 align:middle
around another hike
or holding steady?

12:12.436 --> 12:15.116 align:middle
Is it discussion
around lag times,

12:15.116 --> 12:17.356 align:middle
fear of too much slowing,
too little slowing?

12:17.506 --> 12:19.326 align:middle
Could you walk us through
what this disagreement was

12:19.326 --> 12:20.156 align:middle
about at the meeting?

12:21.526 --> 12:22.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:22.126 --> 12:25.096 align:middle
Yeah. So the proposal at
the meeting was to-was

12:25.096 --> 12:29.406 align:middle
to maintain our current
policy stance, and,

12:29.406 --> 12:32.546 align:middle
and I think there was obviously
unanimous support for that.

12:33.316 --> 12:35.166 align:middle
But this, of course,
is an SEP meeting,

12:35.166 --> 12:36.736 align:middle
and so people write
down what they think.

12:36.736 --> 12:39.966 align:middle
And you've got-you have some-you
saw, I think-seven wrote

12:39.966 --> 12:43.726 align:middle
down no hike at this-at
this meeting-or between now

12:43.726 --> 12:44.436 align:middle
and the end of the year.

12:44.436 --> 12:48.966 align:middle
And I think 12 wrote down
another single hike in one

12:48.966 --> 12:50.676 align:middle
of the next two meetings
that we have

12:50.676 --> 12:51.396 align:middle
between the end of the year.

12:51.396 --> 12:53.216 align:middle
So it wasn't like
we were arguing

12:53.216 --> 12:55.996 align:middle
over that-people just stating
their positions-and, really,

12:55.996 --> 12:57.276 align:middle
what, what people are saying is,

12:57.276 --> 12:58.496 align:middle
"Let's see how the
data come in."

12:58.916 --> 13:00.936 align:middle
You know, we, we want to see-you
know what we want to see.

13:00.936 --> 13:02.656 align:middle
We want to see that, that this,

13:02.806 --> 13:05.466 align:middle
this-these good inflation
readings that we've been seeing

13:05.466 --> 13:07.366 align:middle
for the last three months-we
want to see that it's more

13:07.366 --> 13:08.996 align:middle
than just three months, right?

13:09.446 --> 13:11.966 align:middle
We want to see, you know-the,
the labor market report

13:11.966 --> 13:14.226 align:middle
that we received, the
last one that we received,

13:14.716 --> 13:17.036 align:middle
was a good example of what we
do-of what we do want to see.

13:17.106 --> 13:20.786 align:middle
It was a combination of, of, you
know, of-across a broad range

13:20.786 --> 13:24.116 align:middle
of indicators, continuing
rebalancing of the labor market.

13:24.326 --> 13:27.066 align:middle
So those are the two things-so
those are our two mandate

13:27.066 --> 13:28.186 align:middle
variables, and, and that's,

13:28.356 --> 13:29.896 align:middle
that's the progress
that we want to see.

13:29.896 --> 13:32.406 align:middle
But I think people-they want to
be convinced, you know-they want

13:32.406 --> 13:36.636 align:middle
to be careful to-not to jump to
a conclusion, really, one way

13:36.636 --> 13:39.406 align:middle
or the other-but just be
convinced that the data,

13:39.656 --> 13:41.296 align:middle
you know, support
that conclusion.

13:41.516 --> 13:44.506 align:middle
And that's why, given
how far we've come

13:44.506 --> 13:47.196 align:middle
and how quickly we've come,
we're actually in a position

13:47.196 --> 13:50.416 align:middle
to be able to proceed carefully
as we assess the incoming data

13:50.876 --> 13:52.596 align:middle
and the evolving
outlooks and risks

13:52.596 --> 13:54.286 align:middle
and make these decisions
meeting by meeting.

13:54.536 --> 13:54.836 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

13:54.896 --> 13:55.886 align:middle
I see. And, in your view,

13:55.886 --> 13:58.706 align:middle
what would-I know nothing has
been decided yet-but what would

13:58.706 --> 14:00.916 align:middle
one more hike at the
end of the year do

14:00.916 --> 14:02.266 align:middle
to the economy or to inflation?

14:02.266 --> 14:05.086 align:middle
And on the other side,
what would no hike do,

14:05.086 --> 14:06.566 align:middle
if you could sort of
game that out for us?

14:06.946 --> 14:07.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:07.686 --> 14:09.366 align:middle
So, you know, you, you
can make the argument

14:09.456 --> 14:11.146 align:middle
that one hike one way or
the other won't matter.

14:11.146 --> 14:14.366 align:middle
But, for us, we're, we're
trying-obviously, as a group,

14:14.866 --> 14:18.076 align:middle
it's a pretty tight cluster
of, of where we think that,

14:18.076 --> 14:20.976 align:middle
that policy stance might
be, but we're always going

14:20.976 --> 14:22.036 align:middle
to be learning from data.

14:22.206 --> 14:23.926 align:middle
You know, we've learned
all through the course

14:23.926 --> 14:25.816 align:middle
of the last year that, actually,

14:25.816 --> 14:27.556 align:middle
we needed to go further
than we had thought.

14:27.556 --> 14:28.826 align:middle
If you go back a
year-and what we thought,

14:28.826 --> 14:31.536 align:middle
what we wrote down-it's actually
gotten higher and higher.

14:31.916 --> 14:35.286 align:middle
So we, we don't really know
until-and that's why, again,

14:35.286 --> 14:37.516 align:middle
we-we're in a position to
proceed carefully at this point.

14:37.726 --> 14:41.126 align:middle
A year ago, we proceeded
pretty quickly to get rates up.

14:41.126 --> 14:46.566 align:middle
Now-now we're fairly close, we
think, to where we need to get.

14:46.566 --> 14:49.266 align:middle
It's, it's just a question
of reaching the right stance.

14:49.356 --> 14:53.816 align:middle
I wouldn't attribute huge
importance to one hike in,

14:53.816 --> 14:55.166 align:middle
in macroeconomic terms.

14:55.166 --> 14:59.236 align:middle
Nonetheless, you know, we
need-we need to get to a place

14:59.236 --> 15:02.126 align:middle
where we're confident
that we have a stance

15:02.126 --> 15:04.876 align:middle
that will bring inflation
down to 2 percent over time.

15:05.456 --> 15:07.936 align:middle
That's what we need to get
to, and we've been-you know,

15:07.936 --> 15:08.846 align:middle
we've been moving toward it.

15:08.846 --> 15:10.286 align:middle
As we've gotten closer to it,

15:10.776 --> 15:12.596 align:middle
we've slowed the pace
at which we've moved.

15:12.596 --> 15:13.746 align:middle
I think that was appropriate.

15:14.366 --> 15:16.716 align:middle
And now that we're getting
closer, we, we-again,

15:16.716 --> 15:18.326 align:middle
we have the ability
to proceed carefully.

15:19.526 --> 15:20.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:20.176 --> 15:22.886 align:middle
Thanks. Let's go to Steve.

15:23.426 --> 15:24.266 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

15:24.266 --> 15:24.986 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

15:24.986 --> 15:27.396 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I want to return
to Colby's question here.

15:28.836 --> 15:31.476 align:middle
What is it saying about
the Committee's view

15:31.476 --> 15:34.986 align:middle
of the inflation
dynamic in the economy

15:35.836 --> 15:40.316 align:middle
that you achieve the same
forecast inflation rate

15:40.316 --> 15:43.726 align:middle
for next year but need
another half a point

15:43.726 --> 15:44.756 align:middle
of the funds rate on it?

15:44.756 --> 15:46.416 align:middle
Does it tell you
that-does it tell us

15:46.416 --> 15:47.796 align:middle
that the Committee
believes inflation

15:47.796 --> 15:51.296 align:middle
to be more persistent-requires
more medicine, effectively?

15:51.746 --> 15:54.786 align:middle
And I guess a related
question is, if you're going

15:54.786 --> 15:59.266 align:middle
to project a funds rate
above the longer-run rate

15:59.326 --> 16:03.096 align:middle
for four years in a row, at
what point do we start to think,

16:03.096 --> 16:04.336 align:middle
"Hey, maybe the longer rate

16:04.336 --> 16:06.206 align:middle
or the neutral rate
is actually higher"?

16:06.206 --> 16:06.646 align:middle
Thank you.

16:07.336 --> 16:07.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:07.766 --> 16:12.776 align:middle
So I, I guess I would point
more to-rather than pointing

16:12.776 --> 16:15.776 align:middle
to a sense of inflation
having become more persistent,

16:15.776 --> 16:17.906 align:middle
I wouldn't think-that's
not-we've,

16:17.906 --> 16:20.816 align:middle
we've seen inflation be more
persistent over the course

16:20.816 --> 16:23.386 align:middle
of the past year, but I wouldn't
say that's something that's

16:23.386 --> 16:24.536 align:middle
appeared in the recent data.

16:24.536 --> 16:27.666 align:middle
It's more about stronger
economic activity, I would say.

16:28.116 --> 16:31.476 align:middle
So if, if I had to attribute
one thing-again, we're,

16:31.946 --> 16:33.996 align:middle
we're picking medians
here and trying

16:33.996 --> 16:35.306 align:middle
to attribute one explanation.

16:35.306 --> 16:38.666 align:middle
But I think, broadly, stronger
economic activity means,

16:38.756 --> 16:41.426 align:middle
means rates-we have
to do more with rates,

16:41.766 --> 16:44.266 align:middle
and that's what-that's what
that meaning is, is telling you.

16:44.926 --> 16:49.026 align:middle
In terms of, of what the
neutral rate can be-you know,

16:49.596 --> 16:51.076 align:middle
we-we know it by its works.

16:51.216 --> 16:52.826 align:middle
We only know it by
its works, really.

16:52.826 --> 16:57.716 align:middle
We can't-we can't-you know, the,
the models and, and-that we use,

16:58.286 --> 17:02.606 align:middle
you-ultimately, you only know
when you get there and by,

17:02.716 --> 17:04.116 align:middle
by the way the economy reacts.

17:04.116 --> 17:05.996 align:middle
And, again, that's another
reason why we're-why we're

17:05.996 --> 17:08.986 align:middle
moving carefully now, because,
you know, there are lags here.

17:09.486 --> 17:12.106 align:middle
So it, it may-it may, of course,

17:12.166 --> 17:14.666 align:middle
be that the-that the
neutral rate has risen.

17:14.906 --> 17:18.596 align:middle
You do see people-you don't see
the median moving-but you do see

17:18.596 --> 17:21.226 align:middle
people raising their estimates
of, of the neutral rate,

17:21.226 --> 17:23.136 align:middle
and it's certainly plausible

17:23.136 --> 17:26.976 align:middle
that the neutral rate is higher
than, than the longer-run rate.

17:26.976 --> 17:29.656 align:middle
Remember-what we write down in
the SEP is the longer-run rate.

17:30.306 --> 17:33.376 align:middle
It is certainly possible
that, you know,

17:33.376 --> 17:36.106 align:middle
that the-that the neutral
rate at this moment is higher

17:36.106 --> 17:39.676 align:middle
than that, and that-that's
part of the explanation

17:39.736 --> 17:43.076 align:middle
for why the economy has
been more resilient than,

17:43.076 --> 17:43.986 align:middle
than expected.

17:43.986 --> 17:45.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:45.866 --> 17:48.456 align:middle
Let's go to Howard.

17:48.846 --> 17:50.026 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

17:50.026 --> 17:51.016 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

17:51.016 --> 17:51.366 align:middle
Thank you.

17:51.436 --> 17:55.366 align:middle
So you've said several times
that the economy needed a period

17:55.366 --> 17:56.796 align:middle
of below-trend growth

17:56.986 --> 17:59.466 align:middle
to get inflation
consistently back to 2 percent.

17:59.976 --> 18:03.796 align:middle
You kind of get that in 2024 a
little bit-1.5 percent is just a

18:03.856 --> 18:06.126 align:middle
touch below what's the
estimate of potential.

18:06.616 --> 18:09.266 align:middle
So the fact that you're
getting so much done

18:09.336 --> 18:12.076 align:middle
at so much less cost, does
that represent a change

18:12.076 --> 18:13.616 align:middle
in how you think
inflation works,

18:13.616 --> 18:15.896 align:middle
a change in how you
think the economy works,

18:16.026 --> 18:18.186 align:middle
a change in the mix
of supply healing

18:18.186 --> 18:21.336 align:middle
versus demand destruction that's
necessary to achieve this?

18:21.776 --> 18:22.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:22.236 --> 18:22.906 align:middle
Yes, of course.

18:22.906 --> 18:25.546 align:middle
It is a-it is a good
thing that we,

18:25.626 --> 18:29.266 align:middle
we've seen now meaningful
rebalancing in the labor market

18:29.656 --> 18:31.856 align:middle
without an increase in
unemployment, and that's,

18:31.856 --> 18:35.316 align:middle
that's because we're seeing that
rebalancing in other places-in,

18:35.316 --> 18:40.056 align:middle
for example, job openings
and in the jobs-worker gap.

18:40.056 --> 18:43.746 align:middle
You're also seeing supply-side
things-so, so that's happening.

18:44.616 --> 18:48.316 align:middle
I would say, though, we still-I
still think, and I think,

18:48.406 --> 18:50.206 align:middle
broadly, people still
think, that there will have

18:50.206 --> 18:53.076 align:middle
to be some softening in the
labor market that can come

18:53.076 --> 18:55.586 align:middle
through more supply,
as we've seen as well.

18:55.876 --> 18:57.916 align:middle
Also, remember, the
natural rate, we think, is,

18:58.026 --> 19:01.626 align:middle
is coming down, which is a
supply-side thing, so that the,

19:01.886 --> 19:05.136 align:middle
the gap between any given
unemployment rate that's lower

19:05.136 --> 19:07.576 align:middle
than that and the
natural rate comes down.

19:07.576 --> 19:10.066 align:middle
That's a way for supply-that's
a way for the labor market

19:10.066 --> 19:11.666 align:middle
to achieve a better balance.

19:11.666 --> 19:13.446 align:middle
So all of those things
are happening.

19:13.706 --> 19:16.526 align:middle
You're right-in, in
the median forecast,

19:16.526 --> 19:18.826 align:middle
we don't see a big
increase in unemployment.

19:18.826 --> 19:22.986 align:middle
We do see an increase, and-but
that's-that really is just

19:23.026 --> 19:25.526 align:middle
"playing forward" the trends
that we've been seeing.

19:26.056 --> 19:27.066 align:middle
That is not guaranteed.

19:27.066 --> 19:29.576 align:middle
There, there may come a time
when unemployment goes up more

19:29.576 --> 19:30.416 align:middle
than that, but that's,

19:30.466 --> 19:32.146 align:middle
that's really what
we've been seeing is,

19:32.176 --> 19:34.106 align:middle
progress without higher
unemployment for now.

19:34.376 --> 19:34.876 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

19:34.876 --> 19:37.196 align:middle
So just to, to boil that
down for a second-you know,

19:37.196 --> 19:40.836 align:middle
we've gone from a very narrow
path to a-to a soft landing

19:41.116 --> 19:43.346 align:middle
to something different.

19:43.416 --> 19:47.886 align:middle
Would you call the soft landing
now a baseline expectation?

19:48.816 --> 19:49.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:49.206 --> 19:52.486 align:middle
No, no-I would not do that.

19:52.486 --> 19:55.096 align:middle
I, I would just say-what,
what would I say about that?

19:55.866 --> 19:59.036 align:middle
I've always thought that
the soft landing was,

19:59.206 --> 20:02.606 align:middle
was a plausible outcome-that
there was a path, really,

20:02.856 --> 20:04.486 align:middle
to, to a soft landing.

20:04.486 --> 20:06.736 align:middle
I've thought that, and I've
said that since we lifted off.

20:07.206 --> 20:10.416 align:middle
It's also possible that,
that the path has narrowed,

20:10.416 --> 20:11.896 align:middle
and it's widened, apparently.

20:12.656 --> 20:16.766 align:middle
Ultimately, it-this may
be decided by factors

20:16.766 --> 20:18.746 align:middle
that are outside our control
at the end of the day.

20:18.786 --> 20:21.376 align:middle
But I do think it's-I
do think it's possible.

20:21.656 --> 20:28.016 align:middle
And, you know-I also think,
you know, this is why we're

20:28.016 --> 20:30.786 align:middle
in a position to, to
move carefully again.

20:30.786 --> 20:33.696 align:middle
That-we, we will
restore price stability.

20:33.736 --> 20:35.256 align:middle
We, we know that
we have to do that,

20:35.256 --> 20:37.306 align:middle
and we know the public
depends on us doing that,

20:37.866 --> 20:39.456 align:middle
and we know that
we have to do it

20:39.586 --> 20:42.276 align:middle
so that we can achieve the kind
of labor market that we all want

20:42.276 --> 20:45.756 align:middle
to achieve, which is an
extended period-sustained period

20:45.756 --> 20:47.896 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

20:48.436 --> 20:48.956 align:middle
We know that.

20:48.956 --> 20:54.016 align:middle
The fact that we've come this
far lets us really proceed

20:54.016 --> 20:55.946 align:middle
carefully, as I keep saying.

20:56.036 --> 20:59.086 align:middle
So I think, you know, that's,

20:59.176 --> 21:00.666 align:middle
that's the end we're
trying to achieve.

21:01.336 --> 21:03.876 align:middle
I wouldn't want to handicap
the likelihood of it, though.

21:03.876 --> 21:06.126 align:middle
It's not up to me to do that.

21:06.896 --> 21:08.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:08.286 --> 21:10.166 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

21:11.946 --> 21:13.236 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

21:14.236 --> 21:16.986 align:middle
Chair Powell, both you and Vice
Chair Williams have indicated

21:16.986 --> 21:19.906 align:middle
that "sufficiently restrictive"
will be judged on a real rather

21:19.906 --> 21:22.146 align:middle
than nominal basis,
implying some scope

21:22.676 --> 21:25.346 align:middle
for nominal rate cuts next
year-provided further compelling

21:25.346 --> 21:28.716 align:middle
evidence that price pressures
will continue to subside.

21:29.296 --> 21:33.616 align:middle
Is the FOMC focused on
targeting a real level

21:33.616 --> 21:34.826 align:middle
of policy restriction?

21:35.216 --> 21:37.696 align:middle
And can you explain what would
constitute enough evidence

21:37.696 --> 21:40.996 align:middle
that will allow the FOMC to
normalize the nominal stance

21:40.996 --> 21:43.726 align:middle
of policy while keeping
real policy settings

21:43.766 --> 21:44.846 align:middle
sufficiently restrictive?

21:44.956 --> 21:46.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:46.386 --> 21:48.826 align:middle
I mean, yes-we, we understand
that it's a real rate

21:48.826 --> 21:52.026 align:middle
that will matter and that needs
to be sufficiently restrictive.

21:52.646 --> 21:55.976 align:middle
And, again, I would
say, you know-you know

21:55.976 --> 21:58.946 align:middle
"sufficiently restrictive"
only when you see it.

21:59.186 --> 22:02.326 align:middle
You-it's not something you
can arrive at with confidence

22:02.326 --> 22:04.626 align:middle
in a model or, or in
various estimates, you know.

22:04.896 --> 22:05.986 align:middle
And so what are we seeing?

22:05.986 --> 22:09.436 align:middle
We're, we're seeing, you know,
through a combination of the,

22:09.436 --> 22:12.026 align:middle
you know, the unwinding of
the pandemic-related demand

22:12.026 --> 22:15.556 align:middle
and supply distortions
and monetary policy's work

22:15.676 --> 22:18.196 align:middle
in suppressing demand
or, or, you know,

22:18.196 --> 22:20.866 align:middle
alleviating very high
demand-the combination

22:20.866 --> 22:22.586 align:middle
of those two things
is actually working.

22:23.056 --> 22:25.766 align:middle
You're seeing, you know,
inflation coming down.

22:25.766 --> 22:29.236 align:middle
It's principally now in
goods-also in housing services.

22:29.236 --> 22:32.316 align:middle
You begin to see effects of it
in nonhousing services as well.

22:32.316 --> 22:35.136 align:middle
So I think-we think
that that is working.

22:35.736 --> 22:40.906 align:middle
And I, I think, you
know-as we've said, we,

22:40.956 --> 22:43.506 align:middle
we want to reach that-we
want to reach something

22:43.506 --> 22:46.996 align:middle
that we're confident
gets us to that level.

22:46.996 --> 22:49.756 align:middle
And I think confidence
comes from seeing, you know,

22:50.076 --> 22:52.906 align:middle
enough data that you
feel like, "Yes, okay,

22:52.906 --> 22:55.526 align:middle
this feels like it-we
can-we can, for now,

22:56.376 --> 22:57.786 align:middle
decide that this
is the right level

22:58.186 --> 22:59.486 align:middle
and just agree to stay here."

22:59.486 --> 23:01.606 align:middle
We're not permanently
deciding not to go higher, but,

23:02.106 --> 23:03.346 align:middle
but we would-let's say,

23:03.346 --> 23:04.816 align:middle
if we get to that level-and
then the question is,

23:04.816 --> 23:06.266 align:middle
how long do you stay
at that level?

23:06.826 --> 23:08.366 align:middle
And that's a whole
other set of questions.

23:08.366 --> 23:11.116 align:middle
For now, the question is
trying to find that level

23:12.216 --> 23:13.326 align:middle
where we think we
can stay there.

23:13.586 --> 23:16.056 align:middle
And we haven't-we
haven't gotten to a point

23:16.056 --> 23:17.266 align:middle
of confidence about that yet.

23:18.096 --> 23:20.106 align:middle
That's, that's what
we're-that's,

23:20.106 --> 23:21.006 align:middle
that's the stage
we're at, though.

23:21.636 --> 23:22.286 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

23:22.286 --> 23:24.086 align:middle
But it looks like there
was-because there was an

23:24.086 --> 23:25.066 align:middle
across-the-board drop

23:25.066 --> 23:28.196 align:middle
in the core PCE projection-core
PCE inflation projection

23:28.236 --> 23:31.006 align:middle
for this year, and even
then it seems possible

23:31.436 --> 23:33.986 align:middle
that core PCE inflation
could come in even lower

23:33.986 --> 23:36.656 align:middle
than the median at 3.7 percent.

23:36.716 --> 23:39.296 align:middle
Would you see a case to raise
rates still if it turned

23:39.296 --> 23:40.026 align:middle
out that you were going

23:40.026 --> 23:42.566 align:middle
to achieve the same real rate
this year because the decline

23:42.566 --> 23:44.606 align:middle
in inflation proceeds
somewhat better

23:44.606 --> 23:46.356 align:middle
than you-than you
currently anticipate?

23:46.356 --> 23:46.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:46.916 --> 23:50.096 align:middle
The decision that we make at,
you know, at each meeting and,

23:50.266 --> 23:52.756 align:middle
certainly, at the-at the last
two meetings this year-it's

23:52.756 --> 23:55.156 align:middle
going to depend on the
totality of all the data: so,

23:55.156 --> 23:58.496 align:middle
the inflation data, the labor
market data, the growth data,

23:58.946 --> 24:01.556 align:middle
the, the balance of
risks, and the other events

24:01.556 --> 24:02.436 align:middle
that are happening out there.

24:02.436 --> 24:04.476 align:middle
We'll make-we take all
of that into account,

24:04.476 --> 24:06.726 align:middle
so I can't really
answer a hypothetical

24:06.726 --> 24:07.856 align:middle
about one piece of that.

24:07.856 --> 24:10.536 align:middle
It'll-it'll be trying
to reach a judgment

24:10.536 --> 24:12.526 align:middle
over whether we should
move forward

24:12.526 --> 24:14.546 align:middle
with another rate hike
overall and whether

24:14.546 --> 24:17.626 align:middle
that would increase our
confidence that, that, yes,

24:18.246 --> 24:20.536 align:middle
this is an appropriate
move, and it will help,

24:20.636 --> 24:23.006 align:middle
help us be more confident
that we've gotten to the level

24:23.006 --> 24:23.796 align:middle
that we need to get to.

24:24.826 --> 24:25.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:30.216 --> 24:31.346 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

24:31.346 --> 24:32.186 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

24:32.796 --> 24:36.096 align:middle
Following up on Nick's question,
actually-John Williams,

24:36.456 --> 24:39.996 align:middle
the New York Fed president,
obviously, has said things

24:39.996 --> 24:44.016 align:middle
to the effect of, "Next year, as
we see inflation kind of"-again,

24:44.056 --> 24:46.486 align:middle
to Nick's point-"as, as we
see inflation coming down,

24:46.826 --> 24:48.976 align:middle
we're going to need to reduce
interest rates to make sure

24:48.976 --> 24:50.576 align:middle
that we're not squeezing
the economy harder

24:50.576 --> 24:51.766 align:middle
and harder over time."

24:51.866 --> 24:54.186 align:middle
And I wonder if that's
basically the logic

24:54.186 --> 24:57.116 align:middle
that you apply-you know, is
that how you think about it?

24:57.116 --> 24:59.666 align:middle
And then I also wonder-in
the last press conference,

24:59.666 --> 25:01.406 align:middle
you said something to
the effect of, you know,

25:01.406 --> 25:03.336 align:middle
"It's a full year
out-those discussions,"

25:03.336 --> 25:05.016 align:middle
and people interpreted
that to mean

25:05.016 --> 25:08.396 align:middle
that you didn't see a
possibility of a rate cut

25:08.396 --> 25:09.956 align:middle
in the first half of next year.

25:09.956 --> 25:13.166 align:middle
And I wonder if that was what
you meant by that or whether,

25:13.356 --> 25:14.936 align:middle
you know-how you're
thinking about that timing.

25:15.556 --> 25:16.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:16.106 --> 25:17.866 align:middle
No. When-so when I
answer these questions

25:17.866 --> 25:21.346 align:middle
about hypotheticals about, about
cutting, I'm never intending

25:21.346 --> 25:24.266 align:middle
to send a signal about timing;
I'm just answering them as,

25:24.266 --> 25:25.576 align:middle
as the question is expressed.

25:25.576 --> 25:28.496 align:middle
So, so please-I, I wouldn't
want to be taken that way.

25:28.496 --> 25:29.776 align:middle
Sorry-the first question
was, is that how?

25:30.066 --> 25:32.116 align:middle
Yeah, so we're-as we
go into next year,

25:32.636 --> 25:35.306 align:middle
that's the question we'll
be asking is-you know,

25:35.306 --> 25:38.026 align:middle
taking into account lags and,
and everything else we know

25:38.026 --> 25:40.666 align:middle
about the economy and everything
we know about monetary policy,

25:41.256 --> 25:45.806 align:middle
the, the time will come at some
point, and I'm not saying when,

25:46.076 --> 25:48.186 align:middle
that it's-that it's
appropriate to cut.

25:48.966 --> 25:51.576 align:middle
Part of that may be that
real rates are rising

25:51.576 --> 25:52.766 align:middle
because inflation
is coming down.

25:52.856 --> 25:56.476 align:middle
Part of it just may be
that-it'll be all the factors

25:56.566 --> 25:57.746 align:middle
that we see in the economy.

25:57.746 --> 26:00.076 align:middle
And, you know, that time will
certainly come at some point.

26:00.496 --> 26:02.836 align:middle
And you-what you see is
us writing down, you know,

26:03.146 --> 26:05.696 align:middle
a year ahead, estimates
of what that might be.

26:05.766 --> 26:07.166 align:middle
And, you know, there's-you know,

26:07.166 --> 26:08.826 align:middle
there's so much uncertainty
around that.

26:08.826 --> 26:10.356 align:middle
In, in-when we-in the moment,

26:10.356 --> 26:11.716 align:middle
we'll do what we
think makes sense.

26:11.716 --> 26:14.436 align:middle
No one will look back at this
and say, "Hey, we made a plan."

26:14.956 --> 26:15.946 align:middle
It's not like that at all.

26:15.946 --> 26:18.566 align:middle
It's-this is-these are
estimates made a year in advance

26:18.566 --> 26:21.596 align:middle
that are highly uncertain,
and that's how it is.

26:22.646 --> 26:23.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:23.816 --> 26:24.416 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

26:25.336 --> 26:26.996 align:middle
Thanks so much, Chair Powell.

26:26.996 --> 26:28.106 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

26:28.346 --> 26:29.816 align:middle
I wonder-how do you
think about the question

26:29.816 --> 26:32.706 align:middle
of whether the strong GDP growth
we've been seeing is driven

26:32.706 --> 26:35.446 align:middle
by excess demand versus
supply-side factors,

26:35.646 --> 26:36.976 align:middle
productivity, labor
force growth?

26:37.426 --> 26:40.776 align:middle
And, relatedly, if GDP
keeps coming in "hot,"

26:40.776 --> 26:42.506 align:middle
even in the absence of
inflation resurgence,

26:42.746 --> 26:43.776 align:middle
would that on its
own be a reason

26:43.776 --> 26:44.636 align:middle
to consider more tightening?

26:46.546 --> 26:48.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:48.646 --> 26:52.286 align:middle
So on, on your first question-I
mean, we're looking at GDP very,

26:52.286 --> 26:53.576 align:middle
very carefully to try

26:53.576 --> 26:56.506 align:middle
to understand really
what's the direction

26:56.506 --> 26:58.556 align:middle
of it-what's, what's driving it.

26:58.556 --> 27:00.486 align:middle
And it's, it's a lot of
consumer spending, you know.

27:00.486 --> 27:05.806 align:middle
It's been-the consumer's been
very robust in its-in spending.

27:05.806 --> 27:10.786 align:middle
So that is, you know, that's
how we're looking at it.

27:10.926 --> 27:13.786 align:middle
Sorry, your second
question was- NEIL IRWIN.

27:13.786 --> 27:15.356 align:middle
Does GDP stays "hot,"
but without inflation?

27:15.356 --> 27:16.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:16.046 --> 27:19.086 align:middle
So I, I think the question
will be-GDP is not our mandate,

27:19.146 --> 27:19.356 align:middle
right?

27:19.406 --> 27:21.926 align:middle
Maximum employment and price
stability are the mandates.

27:22.176 --> 27:26.676 align:middle
The question will be, is, is the
level-is GDP-is the-is the heat

27:26.676 --> 27:30.066 align:middle
that we see in GDP-is it
really a threat to our ability

27:30.066 --> 27:31.506 align:middle
to get back to 2
percent inflation?

27:31.716 --> 27:32.706 align:middle
That's going to be the question.

27:33.096 --> 27:35.426 align:middle
It's not-it's not a question
about GDP on its own.

27:35.976 --> 27:37.976 align:middle
It's, you-you know,
you're expecting

27:38.066 --> 27:42.106 align:middle
to see the-this improvement,
you know-continued rebalancing

27:42.106 --> 27:44.946 align:middle
in the labor market and
inflation moving back

27:44.946 --> 27:46.916 align:middle
down to 2 percent in
a sustainable way.

27:46.916 --> 27:49.996 align:middle
We have to have confidence
in that, and, you know,

27:49.996 --> 27:53.496 align:middle
we'd be-we'd be looking at
GDP just, just to the extent

27:53.496 --> 27:56.126 align:middle
that it threatens
one or both of those.

27:57.006 --> 27:58.036 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:58.036 --> 28:00.766 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

28:01.406 --> 28:03.566 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guido
with Politico.

28:03.726 --> 28:05.616 align:middle
There are multiple
external factors

28:05.616 --> 28:06.986 align:middle
that are playing out right now.

28:06.986 --> 28:09.366 align:middle
We see rising oil prices.

28:09.366 --> 28:11.566 align:middle
We see auto workers striking.

28:11.566 --> 28:14.126 align:middle
There's the looming,
very real possibility

28:14.126 --> 28:15.096 align:middle
of a government shutdown.

28:15.096 --> 28:17.476 align:middle
And I was just wondering,
for each of those things,

28:17.786 --> 28:19.746 align:middle
could you talk about how
you're thinking about how

28:19.746 --> 28:22.046 align:middle
that might affect the course
for the Fed and the economy?

28:22.646 --> 28:23.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:23.076 --> 28:25.596 align:middle
So there is a-there is a long
list, and you hit some of them.

28:25.596 --> 28:27.666 align:middle
But it-you know,
it's, it's the strike,

28:27.666 --> 28:29.016 align:middle
it's government shutdown,

28:29.426 --> 28:31.166 align:middle
resumption of student
loan payments,

28:31.716 --> 28:33.986 align:middle
higher long-term
rates, oil price shock.

28:33.986 --> 28:35.206 align:middle
You know, you could-there
are a lot of things

28:35.206 --> 28:38.096 align:middle
that you can-you can look at.

28:38.096 --> 28:40.806 align:middle
And, you know-so, what we try
to do is assess all of them and,

28:40.806 --> 28:42.446 align:middle
and handicap all of them.

28:42.446 --> 28:43.606 align:middle
And, ultimately, though, there's

28:43.606 --> 28:46.186 align:middle
so much uncertainty
around, around these things.

28:46.186 --> 28:47.876 align:middle
I mean, to, to start with
the strike-first of all,

28:47.876 --> 28:52.666 align:middle
we absolutely don't comment on
the strike, as we have no view

28:52.756 --> 28:54.246 align:middle
on the strike one
way or the other.

28:54.246 --> 28:56.346 align:middle
But we, we do have
to make an assessment

28:56.816 --> 28:58.776 align:middle
of its economic effects
to do our jobs.

28:59.336 --> 29:03.516 align:middle
So, you know, the, the thing
about it is-so uncertain.

29:03.516 --> 29:04.146 align:middle
It will depend.

29:04.146 --> 29:06.626 align:middle
The economic effects-it could
affect-you've looked-we've

29:06.626 --> 29:09.486 align:middle
looked back at history-it
could affect economic output,

29:09.676 --> 29:10.796 align:middle
hiring, and inflation.

29:11.396 --> 29:13.206 align:middle
But that's really going to
depend on how broad it is

29:13.206 --> 29:14.596 align:middle
and how long it's sustained for.

29:14.596 --> 29:16.086 align:middle
And we-and then it also depends

29:16.086 --> 29:18.086 align:middle
on how quickly production
can make

29:18.086 --> 29:19.746 align:middle
up for, for lost production.

29:19.746 --> 29:21.466 align:middle
So none of those things
are known right now.

29:21.466 --> 29:23.436 align:middle
It's very, very hard to know.

29:23.436 --> 29:25.176 align:middle
So you just have to
leave that uncertain.

29:25.176 --> 29:27.806 align:middle
And, and we'll be learning,
I think, over the course

29:27.806 --> 29:30.576 align:middle
of the next intermeeting
period, much more about that.

29:30.576 --> 29:31.926 align:middle
And the same is true
for the others.

29:31.926 --> 29:33.826 align:middle
We, we-I don't know if
you mentioned shutdown;

29:33.826 --> 29:35.356 align:middle
I think of all of these
as being on the list.

29:35.756 --> 29:36.776 align:middle
We don't comment on that.

29:36.776 --> 29:40.166 align:middle
It hasn't traditionally had
much of a macroeconomic effect.

29:40.816 --> 29:44.306 align:middle
You know, energy prices
being higher-that's-that is a

29:44.306 --> 29:45.526 align:middle
significant thing.

29:46.456 --> 29:49.806 align:middle
We-energy prices being
up can affect spending.

29:49.806 --> 29:51.816 align:middle
It can affect the
consumer over time.

29:52.066 --> 29:55.556 align:middle
A sustained period of higher-of
higher energy prices can affect

29:55.696 --> 29:57.926 align:middle
consumer expectations
about inflation.

29:58.436 --> 30:00.736 align:middle
We tend to look through
short-term volatility

30:00.736 --> 30:02.476 align:middle
and look at-look
at core inflation.

30:02.926 --> 30:04.556 align:middle
But so the question
is, how long are,

30:04.556 --> 30:06.516 align:middle
are higher prices sustained?

30:06.516 --> 30:09.626 align:middle
We have to-we have to take
those macroeconomic effects

30:09.626 --> 30:10.656 align:middle
into account as well.

30:11.396 --> 30:12.396 align:middle
Those are-those are
some of them.

30:12.396 --> 30:13.556 align:middle
I'm not sure if I hit them all.

30:13.556 --> 30:16.916 align:middle
But-I mean, ultimately, you
know, you're, you're coming

30:16.916 --> 30:18.466 align:middle
into this with an
economy that appears

30:18.466 --> 30:19.726 align:middle
to have significant momentum.

30:20.476 --> 30:22.586 align:middle
And that's, that's
what we start with.

30:22.586 --> 30:24.056 align:middle
And we-but we do
have this collection

30:24.056 --> 30:24.926 align:middle
of risks that you mentioned.

30:25.996 --> 30:27.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:27.416 --> 30:28.896 align:middle
Craig. CRAIG TORRES.

30:28.896 --> 30:30.266 align:middle
Craig Torres from
Bloomberg News.

30:30.266 --> 30:32.596 align:middle
I was a little surprised,
Chair Powell, to hear you say

30:32.596 --> 30:35.796 align:middle
that a soft landing is
not a primary objective.

30:36.476 --> 30:38.916 align:middle
This economy's seeing
added supply in a way

30:39.016 --> 30:41.416 align:middle
that could create long-term
inflation stability.

30:42.036 --> 30:44.676 align:middle
We have prime-age labor
force participation moving

30:44.676 --> 30:46.346 align:middle
up where people can add skills.

30:46.556 --> 30:47.566 align:middle
Workers want to work.

30:48.166 --> 30:50.566 align:middle
We have a boom in
manufacturing construction.

30:51.096 --> 30:53.846 align:middle
We've had a decent
spate of homebuilding.

30:54.376 --> 30:58.366 align:middle
And since inflation's coming
down with strong GDP growth,

30:58.366 --> 31:00.036 align:middle
we may have higher productivity.

31:00.596 --> 31:04.736 align:middle
All are which-good for the
Fed's longer-run target

31:04.736 --> 31:05.856 align:middle
of low inflation.

31:06.086 --> 31:10.686 align:middle
And if we lose that in a
recession, aren't we opting

31:10.726 --> 31:14.196 align:middle
for the awful hysteresis
that we had in 2010?

31:14.806 --> 31:17.966 align:middle
So are you taking this into
account as you pursue policy?

31:18.126 --> 31:18.496 align:middle
Thank you.

31:18.686 --> 31:19.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:19.516 --> 31:23.016 align:middle
To begin: A soft landing
is a primary objective,

31:23.016 --> 31:24.316 align:middle
and I did not say otherwise.

31:25.136 --> 31:27.126 align:middle
I mean, that's, that's
what we've been trying

31:27.126 --> 31:30.046 align:middle
to achieve for all this time.

31:30.826 --> 31:33.536 align:middle
The, the real point,
though, is-the,

31:34.946 --> 31:38.646 align:middle
the worst thing we can do is to
fail to restore price stability,

31:38.786 --> 31:40.826 align:middle
because the record is
clear on that: If you,

31:40.896 --> 31:43.406 align:middle
you don't restore price
stability, inflation comes back,

31:43.796 --> 31:46.046 align:middle
and you go through-you
can have a long period

31:46.686 --> 31:50.556 align:middle
where the economy's
just very uncertain,

31:50.556 --> 31:53.176 align:middle
and it will affect growth; it
will affect all kinds of things.

31:53.176 --> 31:54.806 align:middle
It can be a miserable period

31:54.806 --> 31:58.216 align:middle
to have inflation constantly
coming back and the Fed coming

31:58.216 --> 31:59.566 align:middle
in and having to
tighten again and again.

31:59.896 --> 32:02.776 align:middle
So the best thing we can do
for everyone, we believe,

32:02.776 --> 32:04.196 align:middle
is to restore price stability.

32:04.336 --> 32:08.646 align:middle
I think now, today, we
actually-you know, we,

32:08.696 --> 32:10.786 align:middle
we have the ability to
be careful at this point

32:10.786 --> 32:13.496 align:middle
and move carefully, and that's
what we're planning to do.

32:14.276 --> 32:18.176 align:middle
So we fully appreciate that-you
know, the benefits of being able

32:18.176 --> 32:21.426 align:middle
to continue what we see
already, which is rebalancing

32:21.426 --> 32:23.196 align:middle
in the labor market
and inflation coming

32:23.196 --> 32:27.606 align:middle
down without seeing, you know,
an important, large increase

32:27.846 --> 32:29.546 align:middle
in unemployment,
which has been typical

32:29.546 --> 32:30.536 align:middle
of other tightening cycles.

32:31.246 --> 32:32.226 align:middle
So- MICHELLE SMITH.

32:33.076 --> 32:34.896 align:middle
Let's go to Chris.

32:34.976 --> 32:35.876 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

32:35.876 --> 32:36.816 align:middle
Hi. Thank you.

32:36.956 --> 32:38.556 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

32:39.156 --> 32:40.806 align:middle
When you look at
the disinflation

32:40.806 --> 32:44.176 align:middle
that has taken place so far, do
you see it mostly as a result

32:44.176 --> 32:46.486 align:middle
of what some economists
are calling

32:46.486 --> 32:49.346 align:middle
"the low-hanging
fruit"-such as the unwinding

32:49.346 --> 32:53.256 align:middle
of supply chain snarls and other
pandemic disruptions-or is it

32:53.256 --> 32:58.766 align:middle
more a broad disinflationary
trend that involves most goods

32:58.846 --> 33:00.236 align:middle
and services across the economy?

33:00.736 --> 33:01.016 align:middle
Thank you.

33:01.486 --> 33:01.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:01.696 --> 33:03.846 align:middle
So if I-if I understood
your question,

33:03.846 --> 33:07.566 align:middle
it's-I would say it this
way: I think we knew

33:08.496 --> 33:11.386 align:middle
from the time-from before when
we lifted off, but certainly

33:11.386 --> 33:14.686 align:middle
by the time we lift-we knew
that bringing inflation back

33:14.726 --> 33:19.236 align:middle
down was going to take, as
I call it, the unwinding

33:19.606 --> 33:23.906 align:middle
of these distortions to both
supply and demand that happened

33:23.906 --> 33:25.666 align:middle
because of the pandemic
and the response.

33:25.876 --> 33:27.546 align:middle
So that unwinding was
going to be important.

33:28.046 --> 33:30.476 align:middle
In addition, monetary
policy was going to help.

33:31.126 --> 33:33.536 align:middle
It was going to help
supply side heal by,

33:33.656 --> 33:37.536 align:middle
by cooling demand off
and, just in general,

33:37.536 --> 33:39.386 align:middle
better aligning supply
with demand.

33:39.526 --> 33:41.856 align:middle
So those two forces were
always going to be important.

33:41.996 --> 33:43.996 align:middle
I think it's very hard
to pull them apart.

33:44.066 --> 33:44.866 align:middle
They work together.

33:45.466 --> 33:48.286 align:middle
I, I do think both of them are
at work now, and I think they're

33:48.286 --> 33:50.826 align:middle
at work in a way that
shows you the progress

33:50.826 --> 33:51.996 align:middle
that we-that we are seeing.

33:52.316 --> 33:52.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:53.886 --> 33:54.886 align:middle
Let's go to Mike.

33:54.886 --> 33:55.556 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

33:56.056 --> 33:57.996 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

33:58.596 --> 34:03.616 align:middle
In June, you forecast a 5.6
percent year-end median fed

34:03.616 --> 34:04.756 align:middle
funds rate, and since then,

34:05.156 --> 34:07.386 align:middle
you've more than doubled
your growth forecast.

34:07.386 --> 34:09.956 align:middle
You lowered your unemployment
forecast significantly.

34:09.956 --> 34:12.696 align:middle
So, what would justify
that last move,

34:12.696 --> 34:15.906 align:middle
because the median forecast
is for lower inflation?

34:15.956 --> 34:19.746 align:middle
And given all the known
unknowns that you face,

34:19.846 --> 34:22.566 align:middle
how much confidence do you
have, can investors have,

34:22.566 --> 34:25.076 align:middle
or the American people
have in your forecasts?

34:25.696 --> 34:26.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:26.166 --> 34:28.626 align:middle
Well, forecasts are
highly uncertain.

34:28.876 --> 34:30.236 align:middle
Forecasting is very difficult.

34:30.386 --> 34:33.186 align:middle
Forecasters are a humble lot
with much to be humble about.

34:34.186 --> 34:38.526 align:middle
But to get to, to your question,
though-what's happened is,

34:38.526 --> 34:40.606 align:middle
growth has come in
stronger, right?

34:40.836 --> 34:43.976 align:middle
Stronger than expected-and
that's required higher rates.

34:44.676 --> 34:49.286 align:middle
Unemployment, you
know-you also see that the,

34:49.456 --> 34:51.746 align:middle
the ultimate unemployment
rate is not as high,

34:51.746 --> 34:53.656 align:middle
but that-that's really because
of what we've been seeing

34:53.656 --> 34:54.316 align:middle
in the labor market.

34:54.316 --> 34:57.026 align:middle
We've seen more and more
progress in the labor market

34:57.026 --> 34:59.376 align:middle
without seeing significantly
higher unemployment.

34:59.376 --> 35:00.836 align:middle
So we're, we're continuing
that trend.

35:01.546 --> 35:02.896 align:middle
In terms of inflation, you,

35:02.896 --> 35:06.106 align:middle
you are seeing-the last
three readings are,

35:06.106 --> 35:08.726 align:middle
are very good readings.

35:08.726 --> 35:09.956 align:middle
It's only three readings,
you know.

35:09.956 --> 35:12.346 align:middle
We-we're well aware that we need
to see more than three readings.

35:12.346 --> 35:15.456 align:middle
But if you look at June, July,
and August, you're looking at,

35:15.506 --> 35:18.426 align:middle
you know, really significant
declines in core inflation,

35:18.846 --> 35:21.406 align:middle
largely in the goods
sector-also to some extent

35:21.406 --> 35:24.276 align:middle
in housing services and just a
little in nonhousing services.

35:24.316 --> 35:25.526 align:middle
Those are the three buckets.

35:25.876 --> 35:28.286 align:middle
Headline inflation, of
course, has come way down,

35:28.446 --> 35:30.966 align:middle
largely due to lower
energy prices,

35:31.356 --> 35:32.726 align:middle
some of which is now reversing.

35:33.236 --> 35:35.866 align:middle
So I think-people
should know that,

35:36.056 --> 35:39.836 align:middle
that economic forecasting
is very difficult,

35:39.836 --> 35:41.636 align:middle
and these are highly
uncertain forecasts.

35:42.316 --> 35:44.626 align:middle
But these are-these
are our forecasts.

35:44.626 --> 35:47.806 align:middle
You know, they're, they're-we
have very high-quality people

35:47.806 --> 35:50.306 align:middle
working on these forecasts,
and I think they stand up well

35:50.306 --> 35:51.446 align:middle
against other forecasters.

35:51.446 --> 35:53.176 align:middle
But just the nature
of the business is,

35:53.486 --> 35:55.156 align:middle
the economy is very
difficult to forecast.

35:55.156 --> 35:55.506 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

35:55.506 --> 35:59.006 align:middle
Given the-given the
forecast that you have,

35:59.566 --> 36:04.006 align:middle
what justified not moving today,
and what could justify moving

36:04.006 --> 36:06.456 align:middle
in the future if you think
inflation is coming down?

36:07.296 --> 36:10.166 align:middle
In other words, why did you
leave that extra dot in?

36:10.946 --> 36:12.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:12.306 --> 36:16.576 align:middle
Well, I, I think we have come
very far very fast in, in the,

36:16.576 --> 36:18.576 align:middle
the rate increases
that we've made.

36:19.126 --> 36:20.796 align:middle
And I think it was
important at the beginning

36:20.796 --> 36:22.146 align:middle
that we move quickly,
and we did.

36:22.496 --> 36:26.336 align:middle
And, and I think as we
get closer to the rate

36:26.336 --> 36:28.226 align:middle
that we think-the
stance of monetary policy

36:28.226 --> 36:31.226 align:middle
that we think is
appropriate to bring inflation

36:31.226 --> 36:33.796 align:middle
down to 2 percent
over time, you know,

36:33.796 --> 36:37.256 align:middle
the risks become more two sided,
and the risk of overtightening

36:37.256 --> 36:37.826 align:middle
and the risk of,

36:38.486 --> 36:41.316 align:middle
of undertightening
becomes more equal.

36:41.316 --> 36:44.216 align:middle
And I think the, the natural,
commonsense thing to do is,

36:44.216 --> 36:46.876 align:middle
as you approach that, you
move a little more slowly

36:46.966 --> 36:48.006 align:middle
as you get closer to it.

36:48.006 --> 36:49.056 align:middle
And that, that's
what we're doing.

36:49.056 --> 36:52.086 align:middle
So we're, we're taking
advantage of the fact

36:52.086 --> 36:55.916 align:middle
that we have moved quickly to
move a little more carefully now

36:55.916 --> 37:00.666 align:middle
as we-as we sort of find our
way to, to the right level

37:01.166 --> 37:03.126 align:middle
of restriction that we need

37:03.126 --> 37:04.726 align:middle
to get inflation back
down to 2 percent.

37:04.836 --> 37:06.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:06.156 --> 37:08.216 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

37:09.416 --> 37:10.286 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

37:10.286 --> 37:12.066 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

37:12.776 --> 37:15.456 align:middle
With your focus on
year-over-year PCE,

37:15.566 --> 37:19.006 align:middle
isn't it true that base
effects are huge and that

37:19.006 --> 37:21.736 align:middle
by the time you meet in
November, that it's more likely

37:21.736 --> 37:24.006 align:middle
that you'll have
a low PCE number

37:24.106 --> 37:25.736 align:middle
that would make you
feel more comfortable?

37:26.236 --> 37:29.976 align:middle
And, secondly, how would the
lack of key indicators-like CPI,

37:30.316 --> 37:33.896 align:middle
the jobs report-impact your
approach in upcoming meetings

37:33.896 --> 37:35.726 align:middle
if we were to have a
government shutdown?

37:35.816 --> 37:36.216 align:middle
Thank you.

37:36.216 --> 37:36.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:36.446 --> 37:37.656 align:middle
Sorry-yeah, I missed
the first question.

37:37.656 --> 37:39.306 align:middle
What was-missed what factors?

37:39.306 --> 37:39.786 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

37:39.786 --> 37:40.466 align:middle
The base factors.

37:40.466 --> 37:40.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:40.876 --> 37:41.626 align:middle
Base factors.

37:41.626 --> 37:48.036 align:middle
Ah. Okay. So on that, we're,
you know, we're looking

37:48.036 --> 37:49.606 align:middle
at just monthly, right?

37:49.606 --> 37:51.136 align:middle
You can look at just
monthly readings

37:51.286 --> 37:53.136 align:middle
and see what the increase
was from the prior month.

37:53.786 --> 37:55.636 align:middle
So you're right-when
you go back 3, 6,

37:55.636 --> 37:57.166 align:middle
and 12 months, you
get base factors.

37:57.166 --> 37:59.316 align:middle
But we can-we can
adjust for that.

37:59.896 --> 38:01.756 align:middle
In terms of not getting
data-you know, again,

38:01.756 --> 38:05.196 align:middle
we don't-we don't comment
on government shutdowns.

38:05.556 --> 38:08.116 align:middle
It-it's possible-if, if there
is a government shutdown

38:08.116 --> 38:11.256 align:middle
and it lasts through
the, the next meeting,

38:11.256 --> 38:13.426 align:middle
then it's possible we
wouldn't-we wouldn't be getting

38:13.426 --> 38:16.476 align:middle
some of the data that we would
ordinarily get, and we-you know,

38:16.476 --> 38:17.756 align:middle
we would just have
to deal with that.

38:17.896 --> 38:19.416 align:middle
And I don't know.

38:19.416 --> 38:20.906 align:middle
It's hard for me to
say in advance how

38:20.906 --> 38:22.866 align:middle
that would affect that meeting.

38:22.956 --> 38:24.696 align:middle
It would depend on
all kinds of factors

38:24.696 --> 38:25.606 align:middle
that I don't know about now.

38:25.606 --> 38:28.196 align:middle
But it's certainly a reality
that, that that's a possibility.

38:28.196 --> 38:29.616 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

38:29.616 --> 38:30.806 align:middle
Would you feel more comfortable

38:30.806 --> 38:34.386 align:middle
on the base effects
that-as those kind of fall

38:34.386 --> 38:37.616 align:middle
out of the equation for the next
couple of readings by November,

38:37.616 --> 38:39.026 align:middle
would you feel more
comfortable at that point?

38:39.626 --> 38:40.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:40.136 --> 38:42.096 align:middle
You know, yes.

38:42.096 --> 38:45.516 align:middle
I mean, if you're looking-if-we,

38:45.606 --> 38:47.256 align:middle
we can tell how much
inflation has gone

38:47.256 --> 38:48.516 align:middle
up in a given month, right?

38:48.696 --> 38:50.766 align:middle
And, you know, that's
what we're looking at.

38:50.876 --> 38:52.736 align:middle
And month by month,
what's the reading?

38:53.266 --> 38:58.266 align:middle
And, you know, I think-I think
what we're really looking at is,

38:58.266 --> 39:00.736 align:middle
there's a tendency to
look at, you know, shorter

39:00.736 --> 39:04.296 align:middle
and shorter maturities, but
they're incredibly volatile,

39:04.296 --> 39:05.236 align:middle
and they can be misleading.

39:05.236 --> 39:06.476 align:middle
That's why we look
at 12-month [rates].

39:06.766 --> 39:08.576 align:middle
But I think, in this
situation-where it looks

39:08.576 --> 39:11.276 align:middle
like we've had a bit of a
turn in inflation starting

39:11.276 --> 39:13.536 align:middle
in June-we're also
looking at six months,

39:13.536 --> 39:15.956 align:middle
and even three months, but
really six months' inflation.

39:15.956 --> 39:17.966 align:middle
So you're looking at
it over that period

39:18.426 --> 39:19.646 align:middle
and over longer periods.

39:19.696 --> 39:20.936 align:middle
That-that's the right way to go.

39:20.996 --> 39:23.796 align:middle
And we don't-we don't need
to be in a hurry to-in,

39:23.796 --> 39:25.686 align:middle
in getting to a conclusion
about what to do.

39:25.686 --> 39:27.076 align:middle
We can let the data evolve.

39:27.896 --> 39:30.146 align:middle
So- MICHELLE SMITH.

39:30.146 --> 39:30.686 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

39:30.756 --> 39:33.296 align:middle
Thanks for the question,
Chair Powell.

39:33.296 --> 39:34.636 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

39:34.936 --> 39:36.636 align:middle
So I want to focus
back in on oil prices.

39:37.216 --> 39:39.526 align:middle
We're seeing oil prices,
as you mentioned, move up,

39:39.636 --> 39:41.086 align:middle
and that's pushing
the price of gas.

39:41.086 --> 39:44.366 align:middle
So how does that factor into
your decision to raise rates

39:44.366 --> 39:47.496 align:middle
or not-because in the last two
inflation reports, PCE and CPI,

39:47.536 --> 39:49.506 align:middle
we've seen, the overall
inflation has actually risen?

39:50.636 --> 39:50.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:51.326 --> 39:52.796 align:middle
Right. So, you know,

39:52.796 --> 39:55.116 align:middle
energy prices are very
important for the consumer.

39:55.116 --> 39:57.076 align:middle
This, this can affect
consumer spending.

39:57.546 --> 39:59.436 align:middle
It certainly can affect
consumer sentiment.

39:59.436 --> 40:01.196 align:middle
I mean, gas prices are one
of the big things that,

40:01.236 --> 40:02.776 align:middle
that affects consumer sentiment.

40:03.486 --> 40:06.826 align:middle
It, it really comes down to how
persistent-how sustained these

40:06.826 --> 40:07.736 align:middle
energy prices are.

40:08.036 --> 40:12.226 align:middle
The reason why we look at core
inflation, which excludes food

40:12.226 --> 40:15.916 align:middle
and energy, is that energy
goes up and down like that.

40:15.916 --> 40:18.396 align:middle
And it doesn't-energy,
energy prices mostly,

40:18.396 --> 40:20.456 align:middle
mostly don't contain
much of a signal

40:20.456 --> 40:23.716 align:middle
about how tight the economy is,
and hence don't tell you much

40:23.716 --> 40:25.386 align:middle
about where inflation's
really going.

40:26.016 --> 40:28.506 align:middle
However, we're well aware,
though, that, that, you know,

40:28.506 --> 40:30.696 align:middle
if energy prices
increase and stay high,

40:30.696 --> 40:32.276 align:middle
that'll have an effect
on spending.

40:32.926 --> 40:37.826 align:middle
And, and it may have an effect
on, on consumer expectations

40:37.826 --> 40:38.896 align:middle
of inflation-things like that.

40:38.896 --> 40:40.346 align:middle
That's just things
we have to monitor.

40:40.706 --> 40:41.776 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

40:41.776 --> 40:44.156 align:middle
On the consumer-they're
putting more and more

40:44.156 --> 40:45.216 align:middle
of this on their credit card.

40:45.536 --> 40:48.106 align:middle
The consumer is seeing, you
know, record credit spending.

40:48.106 --> 40:51.676 align:middle
How long do you think the
consumer can manage that debt

40:52.096 --> 40:53.396 align:middle
at higher interest rates now?

40:53.666 --> 40:55.986 align:middle
And are you concerned about a,
a debt bubble related to that?

40:56.306 --> 40:56.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:56.826 --> 40:59.156 align:middle
So, to, to finish
my prior thought,

40:59.156 --> 41:03.106 align:middle
I was saying that's why we tend
to look through energy moves

41:03.696 --> 41:06.406 align:middle
that we-that we can see as,
as short-term volatility.

41:06.886 --> 41:09.786 align:middle
You know, turning to consumer
credit, you know-of course,

41:09.786 --> 41:10.836 align:middle
we watch that carefully.

41:10.836 --> 41:15.546 align:middle
Consumer distress-measures of
distress among consumers were

41:15.546 --> 41:19.416 align:middle
at historic lows quite
recently, you know, after-during

41:19.416 --> 41:20.896 align:middle
and after the, the pandemic.

41:21.556 --> 41:23.126 align:middle
They're now moving
back up to normal.

41:23.126 --> 41:24.906 align:middle
We're, we're watching
that carefully.

41:25.406 --> 41:27.946 align:middle
But, at this point, these,
these readings are not

41:27.946 --> 41:29.626 align:middle
at troublingly high levels.

41:29.626 --> 41:30.936 align:middle
They're, they're just
kind of moving back

41:30.936 --> 41:33.976 align:middle
up to what was the typical
[level] in the pre-pandemic era.

41:34.466 --> 41:35.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:35.726 --> 41:39.416 align:middle
Jean. JEAN YUNG.

41:39.416 --> 41:42.426 align:middle
Hi. Jean Yung with
MNI Market News.

41:42.566 --> 41:44.506 align:middle
Yields along the
Treasury curve have risen

41:44.556 --> 41:45.966 align:middle
to their highest in years.

41:46.416 --> 41:48.966 align:middle
What is the Fed's view
about what's been driving

41:48.966 --> 41:51.576 align:middle
that increase in recent
weeks, and how much

41:51.576 --> 41:54.976 align:middle
of it can be attributed to
macro explanations-and how much

41:54.976 --> 41:55.776 align:middle
to technical factors?

41:55.776 --> 41:57.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:59.126 --> 42:00.566 align:middle
So you're right.

42:00.816 --> 42:03.376 align:middle
You know, rates have
moved up significantly.

42:03.446 --> 42:08.526 align:middle
I think it's always hard to
say precisely, but it's-most,

42:08.526 --> 42:11.476 align:middle
most people do a common
decomposition of the increase,

42:11.476 --> 42:12.956 align:middle
and they'll, they'll-the
view will be,

42:12.956 --> 42:15.536 align:middle
it's not-it's not mostly
about inflation expectations.

42:16.256 --> 42:20.096 align:middle
It's mostly about other things,
you know-either term premium

42:20.096 --> 42:22.676 align:middle
or real yields-and it's, it's
hard to be precise about this.

42:22.676 --> 42:25.566 align:middle
Of course, everyone's got models
that'll give you a very precise

42:25.566 --> 42:27.416 align:middle
answer, but they give
you different answers.

42:27.416 --> 42:31.346 align:middle
So-but, essentially,
they're, they're moving

42:31.346 --> 42:33.216 align:middle
up because-it's not
because of inflation.

42:33.216 --> 42:36.306 align:middle
It's because probably-it'll
probably have something to do

42:36.306 --> 42:38.606 align:middle
with stronger growth,
I would say-more,

42:38.606 --> 42:40.216 align:middle
more supply of Treasuries.

42:40.216 --> 42:42.156 align:middle
You know, the common
explanations that you hear

42:42.156 --> 42:44.076 align:middle
in the markets kind
of make sense.

42:46.846 --> 42:47.556 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:48.096 --> 42:50.676 align:middle
Kyle. KYLE CAMPBELL.

42:51.696 --> 42:53.056 align:middle
Kyle Campbell, American Banker.

42:53.056 --> 42:56.216 align:middle
Thank you for taking the
questions-just two on,

42:56.286 --> 42:56.966 align:middle
on housing.

42:57.726 --> 43:00.856 align:middle
You've said slower shelter
cost growth is in the pipeline

43:00.856 --> 43:03.236 align:middle
and will reflect in
inflation readings

43:03.326 --> 43:06.456 align:middle
as new leases are signed, but
there's also some questions

43:06.526 --> 43:08.766 align:middle
out there about the way
housing costs are measured,

43:09.146 --> 43:12.436 align:middle
particularly the use of rental
equivalents, which are estimates

43:12.436 --> 43:14.976 align:middle
from homeowners about what
their homes would rent

43:14.976 --> 43:16.636 align:middle
for if they were in
the rental market.

43:17.216 --> 43:20.486 align:middle
So my question is, how much of
the effort to tame inflation,

43:20.826 --> 43:23.516 align:middle
both as it's measured and
felt by the broader public,

43:23.826 --> 43:25.106 align:middle
hinges on housing supply?

43:25.936 --> 43:30.146 align:middle
And then as far as a constrained
housing supply being sort

43:30.146 --> 43:32.646 align:middle
of exacerbated by this
sort of lock-in effect

43:32.996 --> 43:34.966 align:middle
of mortgages being
higher now than they were

43:35.566 --> 43:37.896 align:middle
at their recent historic
lows, how is that going

43:37.896 --> 43:41.216 align:middle
to impact future thinking
about taking interest rates

43:41.326 --> 43:42.816 align:middle
to that lower bound
in the future?

43:42.816 --> 43:43.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:43.816 --> 43:47.456 align:middle
So on the supply point-of
course, supply is very important

43:47.456 --> 43:51.756 align:middle
over time in, in setting
house prices and, and,

43:51.756 --> 43:52.646 align:middle
for that matter, rents.

43:52.646 --> 43:56.386 align:middle
And so-and supply is kind
of structurally constrained.

43:56.996 --> 43:59.546 align:middle
But in terms of, of
where inflation's going

43:59.546 --> 44:02.526 align:middle
in the near term, though,
as, as you obviously know,

44:02.956 --> 44:05.946 align:middle
a lot of it is, is leases
that are running off

44:06.186 --> 44:10.406 align:middle
and then being re-signed or
re-leased at a level that's,

44:10.486 --> 44:14.036 align:middle
that's not as-it won't
be that much higher.

44:14.036 --> 44:16.766 align:middle
A year ago, it would
have been much higher

44:16.766 --> 44:17.796 align:middle
than it was a year before;

44:17.796 --> 44:20.426 align:middle
now it may be below
or at the same level.

44:20.426 --> 44:23.246 align:middle
So as those leases are
rolling over, we're,

44:23.246 --> 44:24.516 align:middle
we're seeing what we expect,

44:24.516 --> 44:29.676 align:middle
which is measured housing
services inflation coming down.

44:30.346 --> 44:33.906 align:middle
Your second question
was the lock-ins.

44:33.906 --> 44:36.556 align:middle
How much is that
affecting things, really?

44:37.016 --> 44:37.286 align:middle
KYLE CAMPBELL.

44:37.316 --> 44:38.896 align:middle
Is that going to
affect your decisions

44:39.096 --> 44:43.586 align:middle
to potentially bring rates
down to their lower bound

44:43.586 --> 44:46.556 align:middle
in the future, sort of creating
that sort of bubble of buying

44:46.556 --> 44:49.546 align:middle
and then a lock-in that sort of
stagnates the housing market?

44:49.546 --> 44:50.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:50.586 --> 44:53.636 align:middle
I, I think we look at the-I
would look at the lock-in, the,

44:53.636 --> 44:55.566 align:middle
the idea being that
people are in,

44:55.566 --> 44:58.336 align:middle
in very low mortgage-very
low-rate mortgages,

44:58.956 --> 45:02.416 align:middle
and if-even if they want to
move now, they-it would be hard

45:02.416 --> 45:04.266 align:middle
because the new mortgage
would be so expensive.

45:04.996 --> 45:07.206 align:middle
And that's explaining
some of the-that's one

45:07.206 --> 45:08.876 align:middle
of the explanations for
what's happening broadly

45:08.876 --> 45:09.586 align:middle
in the labor market.

45:09.826 --> 45:12.626 align:middle
Would that play a role in,

45:13.156 --> 45:17.436 align:middle
in our future decisions-in
a-in a future tight-in a future

45:17.436 --> 45:20.896 align:middle
loosening cycle about
whether we would cut rates?

45:20.986 --> 45:22.986 align:middle
No, I, I don't think it would.

45:23.066 --> 45:26.466 align:middle
I mean, I don't think that's-I,
I think we'd be looking at,

45:26.606 --> 45:28.626 align:middle
what, you know, fundamentally,
what,

45:28.776 --> 45:30.336 align:middle
what rates does the
economy need?

45:30.336 --> 45:32.916 align:middle
And, and, you know, in, in an
emergency like the pandemic

45:32.916 --> 45:36.936 align:middle
or during the Global Financial
Crisis, you, you know, the,

45:36.936 --> 45:40.096 align:middle
the-you have to cut rates to
the point that-you have to do,

45:40.096 --> 45:43.296 align:middle
do what you can to
support the economy.

45:43.296 --> 45:45.726 align:middle
So I wouldn't-I wouldn't think
that that would be a reason

45:45.726 --> 45:46.816 align:middle
for us not to do that.

45:46.876 --> 45:48.766 align:middle
It's not something we're
thinking about at all right now.

45:48.766 --> 45:50.446 align:middle
But, down the road,
I wouldn't think so.

45:51.736 --> 45:52.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:52.116 --> 45:53.766 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

45:53.846 --> 45:56.566 align:middle
Hi. Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

45:56.816 --> 45:58.846 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've
mentioned several things

45:58.886 --> 46:01.636 align:middle
that would possibly weigh
on consumer confidence,

46:01.636 --> 46:05.046 align:middle
maybe cut back consumer
spending-possible government

46:05.046 --> 46:06.606 align:middle
shutdown, high gas prices.

46:07.026 --> 46:09.806 align:middle
At this point, would the
Fed welcome a decrease

46:09.806 --> 46:10.946 align:middle
in consumer spending?

46:10.946 --> 46:13.456 align:middle
Would that help you
get inflation closer

46:13.456 --> 46:14.566 align:middle
to your 2 percent target?

46:15.516 --> 46:16.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:16.116 --> 46:17.016 align:middle
I, I wouldn't say it that way.

46:17.016 --> 46:20.366 align:middle
We're not looking for a
decrease in consumer spending.

46:21.216 --> 46:23.026 align:middle
It's, it's a good thing
that the economy's strong.

46:23.026 --> 46:25.526 align:middle
It's a good thing that the
economy has been able to hold

46:25.526 --> 46:27.916 align:middle
up under, under the
tightening that we've done.

46:28.366 --> 46:29.856 align:middle
It's a good thing that
the labor market's strong.

46:30.316 --> 46:33.836 align:middle
The, the only concern-it,
it just means this:

46:33.946 --> 46:36.826 align:middle
If the economy comes in
stronger than expected,

46:37.146 --> 46:40.016 align:middle
that just means we'll have to do
more in terms of monetary policy

46:40.016 --> 46:42.856 align:middle
to get back to 2 percent-because
we will get back to 2 percent.

46:42.856 --> 46:44.026 align:middle
Does that answer your question?

46:44.026 --> 46:45.346 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

46:45.346 --> 46:48.356 align:middle
Yeah. And I guess, on the
other hand, would you worry

46:48.356 --> 46:49.476 align:middle
that that could contribute

46:49.476 --> 46:51.686 align:middle
to an economic slowdown
or even a recession?

46:52.806 --> 46:53.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:53.576 --> 46:55.086 align:middle
Well, that's always a concern.

46:55.316 --> 46:59.166 align:middle
I mean, concern number one is,
is restoring price stability,

46:59.166 --> 47:02.096 align:middle
because in the long run, that's,
that's something we have to do

47:02.096 --> 47:05.116 align:middle
so that we can have the kind
of economy we really want,

47:05.116 --> 47:07.206 align:middle
which is one with
sustained period

47:07.206 --> 47:09.206 align:middle
of tight labor market
conditions that benefit all,

47:09.206 --> 47:10.556 align:middle
as I-as I've said
a couple times.

47:11.116 --> 47:20.196 align:middle
So that, that said-of course,
you know-we, we also now,

47:20.556 --> 47:23.426 align:middle
given how far we've
come with our rate hikes

47:23.426 --> 47:25.656 align:middle
and how quickly we've come
here, we do have the ability

47:25.656 --> 47:28.086 align:middle
to be careful as we move forward
because of that consideration.

47:28.616 --> 47:29.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:31.336 --> 47:32.896 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

47:32.896 --> 47:33.706 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

47:33.706 --> 47:35.886 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

47:35.886 --> 47:38.626 align:middle
One of the factors in
the economic resilience

47:38.626 --> 47:40.986 align:middle
to date appears to
be a lesser degree

47:41.146 --> 47:42.826 align:middle
of rate sensitivity
than in the past.

47:43.216 --> 47:44.656 align:middle
Obviously, we've
talked about households

47:44.656 --> 47:46.706 align:middle
with long fixed-rate
mortgages-also companies

47:46.706 --> 47:49.236 align:middle
that refinanced before
last year.

47:50.016 --> 47:53.716 align:middle
What is your thinking
about the efficacy of rates

47:53.716 --> 47:54.836 align:middle
and how that's changed?

47:55.186 --> 47:58.026 align:middle
And then, related to
that, how do you think

47:58.026 --> 48:00.166 align:middle
about the distributional
consequences in the sense

48:00.166 --> 48:02.746 align:middle
that if you're a
relatively wealthy household

48:02.746 --> 48:05.966 align:middle
with a long fixed-rate mortgage,
the past year has not been all

48:05.966 --> 48:08.596 align:middle
that tough with rates going
up, whereas if you're relying

48:08.596 --> 48:11.706 align:middle
on your credit card for
supporting your consumption,

48:11.706 --> 48:14.506 align:middle
in fact, times are getting a
lot tougher a lot more quickly?

48:15.176 --> 48:16.746 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

48:17.176 --> 48:20.796 align:middle
So, what-I guess it's
fair-it's fair to say

48:20.796 --> 48:24.256 align:middle
that the economy has been
stronger than many expected,

48:24.526 --> 48:26.676 align:middle
given, given what's
been happening with,

48:26.716 --> 48:27.446 align:middle
with interest rates.

48:27.446 --> 48:28.136 align:middle
Why is that?

48:28.986 --> 48:32.996 align:middle
Many candidate
explanations-possibly-a number

48:32.996 --> 48:33.766 align:middle
of them make sense.

48:33.766 --> 48:36.106 align:middle
One, one is just that
household balance sheets

48:36.616 --> 48:38.456 align:middle
and business balance
sheets have been stronger

48:39.156 --> 48:42.576 align:middle
than we had understood,
and so that,

48:42.576 --> 48:44.726 align:middle
that spending has held
up-and that kind of thing.

48:45.206 --> 48:46.216 align:middle
We're not sure about that.

48:46.216 --> 48:48.876 align:middle
The savings rate for
consumers has come down a lot.

48:48.966 --> 48:50.536 align:middle
The question is whether
that's sustainable.

48:50.866 --> 48:52.826 align:middle
That could-it could just
mean that the-that the date

48:52.826 --> 48:55.406 align:middle
of effect is, is later.

48:56.046 --> 48:59.146 align:middle
It could also be that for
other reasons, the neutral rate

48:59.676 --> 49:03.966 align:middle
of interest is, is higher
for-for various reasons.

49:04.036 --> 49:04.656 align:middle
We don't know that.

49:04.846 --> 49:06.046 align:middle
It could also just be

49:06.456 --> 49:08.246 align:middle
that policy hasn't
been restrictive enough

49:08.296 --> 49:10.346 align:middle
for long enough.

49:10.346 --> 49:13.246 align:middle
And it's-there are many
candidate explanations.

49:13.246 --> 49:16.826 align:middle
We have to, in, in all this
uncertainty, make policy, and I,

49:16.826 --> 49:20.756 align:middle
you know, I feel like what we
have right now is what's still a

49:20.756 --> 49:23.276 align:middle
very strong labor market-but
that's coming back into balance.

49:23.276 --> 49:24.926 align:middle
We were making progress
on inflation.

49:25.666 --> 49:26.726 align:middle
Growth is strong.

49:26.996 --> 49:31.086 align:middle
But I think by many
forecasts-many, many,

49:31.086 --> 49:33.416 align:middle
many forecasts call
for growth to,

49:33.416 --> 49:35.346 align:middle
to moderate over the
course of the next year.

49:35.346 --> 49:38.056 align:middle
So that's where we are, and,
you know, we have to-we have

49:38.056 --> 49:39.066 align:middle
to deal with what comes.

49:39.866 --> 49:41.966 align:middle
On your second question,
which was-sorry,

49:41.966 --> 49:43.356 align:middle
your second question
was distributional,

49:43.356 --> 49:46.066 align:middle
but can you-can you be a
little clearer about that?

49:46.936 --> 49:48.686 align:middle
SIMON RABINOVITCH.

49:48.886 --> 49:50.916 align:middle
Yeah. My point there was
that if you're somebody

49:50.916 --> 49:53.616 align:middle
who has a long fixed-rate
mortgage, you've been able

49:53.616 --> 49:55.856 align:middle
to endure the higher
rates relatively easily.

49:56.216 --> 49:58.036 align:middle
If you're somebody who's
living month to month off

49:58.036 --> 50:00.926 align:middle
of your credit card,
current financing rates are,

50:00.926 --> 50:01.296 align:middle
are punitive.

50:01.296 --> 50:01.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:02.936 --> 50:08.136 align:middle
Yes. And so the point I would
make there is that we're trying

50:08.136 --> 50:09.226 align:middle
to get inflation back down.

50:09.556 --> 50:12.716 align:middle
The people who are most hurt
by inflation are the people

50:12.716 --> 50:13.666 align:middle
who are on a fixed income.

50:14.036 --> 50:17.096 align:middle
If you're a person who
spends all of your income,

50:17.716 --> 50:19.266 align:middle
you don't really have
any meaningful savings.

50:19.266 --> 50:22.646 align:middle
You spend all your income on the
basics of life-clothing, food,

50:22.806 --> 50:27.836 align:middle
transportation, heating-the
basics-and prices go up by 5, 6,

50:27.836 --> 50:31.326 align:middle
7 percent, you're in trouble
right away, whereas even,

50:31.326 --> 50:33.256 align:middle
even middle-class
people have some savings

50:33.256 --> 50:34.496 align:middle
and some ability to absorb that.

50:34.496 --> 50:38.996 align:middle
So it is for those people as
much as for anybody that we need

50:38.996 --> 50:42.206 align:middle
to restore price stability,
and, and we, we want to do it

50:42.206 --> 50:43.926 align:middle
as quickly as possible.

50:44.416 --> 50:47.106 align:middle
Obviously, we-we'd
like to do that.

50:47.106 --> 50:48.786 align:middle
We'd like the current
trend to continue,

50:48.786 --> 50:53.326 align:middle
which is that we're making
progress without seeing the kind

50:53.386 --> 50:56.326 align:middle
of increase in unemployment that
we've seen past-in past things.

50:56.326 --> 50:57.946 align:middle
But you're right, though.

50:57.946 --> 51:00.806 align:middle
When we raise rates,
people who are, you know,

51:00.806 --> 51:02.296 align:middle
living on credit cards and,

51:02.296 --> 51:04.336 align:middle
and borrowing are going
to feel that more.

51:04.426 --> 51:06.586 align:middle
They are. And, of
course, people with,

51:06.586 --> 51:10.196 align:middle
with lots of savings also have
a-have a much lower marginal

51:10.196 --> 51:11.296 align:middle
propensity to consume,

51:11.296 --> 51:13.146 align:middle
and so they're not
going to-it's not going

51:13.146 --> 51:14.336 align:middle
to affect them as much.

51:15.086 --> 51:15.866 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:15.986 --> 51:19.976 align:middle
Let's go to Greg Robb
for the last question.

51:20.256 --> 51:20.566 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

51:21.776 --> 51:22.766 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

51:23.576 --> 51:24.736 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

51:25.716 --> 51:29.516 align:middle
In the Beige Book
recently, you can tell

51:29.516 --> 51:33.376 align:middle
that the Fed has been surveying
nonprofits and community groups

51:33.866 --> 51:35.326 align:middle
about the economic health

51:35.326 --> 51:37.726 align:middle
of low-income
Americans-moderate-income

51:37.786 --> 51:38.196 align:middle
Americans.

51:38.196 --> 51:40.126 align:middle
I have two questions about this.

51:41.326 --> 51:44.146 align:middle
Are you going to use that data
to maybe come up with sort

51:44.146 --> 51:45.716 align:middle
of like a quarterly survey

51:45.786 --> 51:48.626 align:middle
of those groups-like the
senior loan officer survey?

51:49.446 --> 51:53.206 align:middle
And from your-and also
the second question is,

51:53.326 --> 51:57.096 align:middle
from your recent look-readings
of these surveys, how are low

51:57.096 --> 51:58.826 align:middle
and moderate Americans doing?

51:59.066 --> 52:01.986 align:middle
Is there this thing where,
like, the GDP is strong

52:01.986 --> 52:05.196 align:middle
because of wealthy Americans
kind of driving things?

52:05.196 --> 52:08.536 align:middle
I just want to get your sense
of the health of that sector.

52:08.536 --> 52:08.836 align:middle
Thank you.

52:08.836 --> 52:09.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:09.116 --> 52:11.536 align:middle
So I, I don't know about
the quarterly survey.

52:11.536 --> 52:14.686 align:middle
That's an idea we can-we can
take away and think about.

52:14.686 --> 52:18.906 align:middle
In terms of how low and
moderate Americans-you know,

52:18.906 --> 52:21.856 align:middle
they're clearly-were suffering
from, from high inflation.

52:22.286 --> 52:27.676 align:middle
I think during the pandemic,
the, the government transfers

52:27.676 --> 52:31.106 align:middle
that happened were
very meaningful.

52:31.106 --> 52:32.826 align:middle
And, you know, if you
know the-the surveys

52:32.826 --> 52:34.506 align:middle
that we take showed
that-showed that low-

52:34.506 --> 52:36.396 align:middle
and moderate-income people
were actually in very,

52:36.396 --> 52:37.786 align:middle
very strong financial condition.

52:38.456 --> 52:40.806 align:middle
I think now it's a very
"hot" labor market,

52:41.496 --> 52:44.136 align:middle
and you're seeing high
[growth in] nominal wages,

52:44.716 --> 52:46.786 align:middle
and you're starting to see [that
growth rates of] real wages are

52:46.786 --> 52:48.906 align:middle
now positive by most-by
most measures.

52:48.906 --> 52:53.436 align:middle
So I, I think, overall,
households are in good shape.

52:54.136 --> 52:55.736 align:middle
Surveys are a different thing.

52:55.886 --> 52:58.776 align:middle
So surveys are showing
dissatisfaction,

52:59.636 --> 53:01.056 align:middle
and I think a lot
of that is just,

53:01.056 --> 53:03.106 align:middle
people hate inflation-hate it.

53:03.766 --> 53:06.996 align:middle
And that, that causes people to
say, "The economy's terrible."

53:06.996 --> 53:08.446 align:middle
But, at the same time,
they're spending money.

53:08.446 --> 53:09.286 align:middle
Their behavior is,

53:09.336 --> 53:11.766 align:middle
is not exactly what you would
expect from the surveys.

53:12.486 --> 53:16.106 align:middle
That's kind of a, a guess
at what the answer might be.

53:16.106 --> 53:18.876 align:middle
But I, I think there's a
lot of good things happening

53:18.876 --> 53:20.816 align:middle
on household balance
sheets-and, certainly,

53:20.816 --> 53:22.336 align:middle
in the labor market
and with wages.

53:22.766 --> 53:24.566 align:middle
The biggest wage
increases having gone

53:24.596 --> 53:28.206 align:middle
to relatively low-wage jobs-and
now [with] inflation coming

53:28.206 --> 53:29.636 align:middle
down-you're seeing
[increases in] real wages,

53:30.246 --> 53:34.896 align:middle
which is a good thing.

53:38.336 --> 53:53.136 align:middle
Thanks very much.

